# Use available skills when relevant

Before implementing, check whether a skill or plugin applies. If there is even a small chance one fits, read and follow it.

## Project skills (`.cursor/skills/`)

- `frontend-design` — UI, landing/hero, branding, typography, visual layout
- `ui-ux-pro-max` — Design intelligence for professional apps (styles, palettes, Flutter/mobile stacks). Prefer this for Flutter iOS/Android UI/UX.

## Plugin skills (use when the domain matches)

- **Superpowers:** `brainstorming`, `writing-plans`, `executing-plans`, `test-driven-development`, `systematic-debugging`, `verification-before-completion`, `using-git-worktrees`, `finishing-a-development-branch`, `requesting-code-review`, `receiving-code-review`, `subagent-driven-development`, `dispatching-parallel-agents`
- **Supabase:** `supabase`, `supabase-postgres-best-practices`
- **Datadog:** `ddsetup`, `ddconfig`, `ddtoolsets`
- **Browserbase:** `functions`
- **Cursor SDK:** `cursor-sdk` / `sdk`
- **CLI for Agents:** `cli-for-agents`
- **Agent Compatibility:** `check-agent-compatibility`

## Built-in Cursor skills

`automate`, `babysit`, `canvas`, `create-hook`, `create-rule`, `create-skill`, `loop`, `split-to-prs`, `statusline`, `update-cursor-settings`, `review-bugbot`, `review-security`

Do not invent parallel workflows when a skill already covers the task.
